WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
BIG TECH CULTURE:
It’s no secret that big tech companies pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:08.160
out compensation figures that aren’t comparable&nbsp;
to any other industry. Engineers fresh out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.320
college are able to earn nearly $200,000. With&nbsp;
just a couple of years of experience, they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:20.040
up that to $300 to $400,000. And by the time their&nbsp;
in their 30s, they’re often earning $500,000 to $1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:26.440
million per year. These jobs also often come with&nbsp;
a bunch of perks like unlimited paid time off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.280
the ability to work from anywhere in the world,&nbsp;
free food, stock in the fastest growing companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.400
and so on. If you ask the average person, the only&nbsp;
real downside of working at big tech is that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.720
may be some level of moral compromise. I mean,&nbsp;
at this point, it’s really no secret how a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.560
of these companies make money. Even when it’s not&nbsp;
straight up data collection and privacy intrusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:55.440
it’s often monopolization, anti consumer policies,&nbsp;
and abusing of power. But, this moral dilemma is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.720
only the tip of the iceberg, and is often&nbsp;
the least of your concerns when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.360
surviving and advancing at big tech. The reality&nbsp;
is that despite their inviting looking offices and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.680
seemingly employee friendly policies, working at&nbsp;
big tech is much more like being a professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.720
athlete. If you follow sports, you’ll often hear&nbsp;
about contracts that are worth tens of millions if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.800
not hundreds of millions of dolalrs. But, actually&nbsp;
cashing in on the full value of these contracts is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.920
easier said than done. Usually, up half or more of&nbsp;
these contracts are paid out based on performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.280
and unless you’re able to keep up world&nbsp;
class performance for 5 or 10 years straight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.480
you’re only can walk away with a fraction of the&nbsp;
top line value. It’s not that dissimilar at big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:44.800
tech. You get paid sky high salaries, but you’re&nbsp;
also expected to perform at world class levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.760
The moment that you don’t, you’re cut from the&nbsp;
team. And this isn’t speculation or anecdotal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.520
either. You can go onto to Netflix’s official&nbsp;
company culture page and you’ll see them say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:01.040
quote “We model ourselves on being a professional&nbsp;
sports team, not a family. A dream team is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.560
pushing yourself to be the best possible&nbsp;
teammate, caring intensely about your team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.360
and knowing that you may not be on the team&nbsp;
forever. Dream teams are about performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.840
not seniority or tenure. It is up to the manager&nbsp;
to ensure that every player is amazing at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.040
position, plays effectively with others and&nbsp;
is given new opportunities to develop. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:28.560
how we keep winning the championship.” So,&nbsp;
here’s the truth about working at big tech.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:36.840
THE PRESSURE COOKER:
Out of all the big tech companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.760
the company with the most cutthroat environment&nbsp;
is no doubt Netflix. And before we discuss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.000
innerworkings of Netflix, I wanted to clarify that&nbsp;
I’m not trying to paint any of these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.480
in a bad light. Netflix, for example, is often&nbsp;
the most generous of any company in the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.760
world when it comes to comp. You know those multi&nbsp;
six figure and 7 figure contracts that you hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.280
about at big tech companies. Well, most of them&nbsp;
are largely made up of stock comp and bonuses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:11.040
At Netflix, however, the entire comp is purely&nbsp;
cash. So, it’s no wonder why they expect so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.840
and I’m not criticizing that by any means.&nbsp;
Rather, I want to show how working at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.640
companies is often not as rosy as you might&nbsp;
imagine and that there is definitely a price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.720
to pay. With that being said, let’s jump into&nbsp;
the Netflix pressure cooker starting with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:31.520
policy of extreme openness and quote on quote&nbsp;
“radical transparency”. At most companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.280
compensation and performance is something that is&nbsp;
discussed behind closed doors with just your boss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:41.400
and maybe your boss’ boss. This is designed to&nbsp;
minimize conflicts between employees regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:48.520
compensation and keep performance reviews private.&nbsp;
At Netflix, it’s the exact opposite. Everyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:54.040
is director or above has access to the salaries&nbsp;
not just their direct reports but everyone at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.040 --> 00:04:00.000
entire company. And as for performance reviews,&nbsp;
these are even more accessible. Everyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.080
is an admin or up has access to the performance&nbsp;
reviews of everyone at the entire organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.360
You’re probably thinking that this would lead&nbsp;
to a lot of awkward conversations. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.400
always knowing how your peers are perfomring and&nbsp;
how much their earning and that they know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.360
same about you, but that’s kind of the point.&nbsp;
Netflix hopes that by making this information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:25.280
widely accessible, it encourages employees to&nbsp;
constantly improve to survive. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.360
if you know that you’re the worst performing&nbsp;
director while getting paid the most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:35.080
you know that it’s time to step the hell up unless&nbsp;
you wanna be cut next quarter. And believe me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:40.280
Netflix doesn’t think twice when it comes&nbsp;
to firing people, and no one is “safe” which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.600
brings us into the keeper test. Netflix encourages&nbsp;
their management to constantly evaluate whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.160
they would rehire and/or fight for their&nbsp;
direct reports. If the answer is ever no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:56.320
it’s time to fire them. And don’t think that you&nbsp;
can be the chill manager that doesn’t do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.280
either. If you’re the chill manager, you’ll come&nbsp;
off as being soft and soon find yourself on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:07.320
chopping block because like I said, no one is safe&nbsp;
and this applies to even C level executives. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.040
you’ve been a Netflix subscriber for a while, you&nbsp;
probably remember how garbage their recommendation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:17.160
algorithm was back in the mid 2010s. Some&nbsp;
of you would argue that it’s still garbage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:23.640
but it has gotten a lot better and that’s thanks&nbsp;
to the keeper test. In 2017, after receiving a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.680
bunch of negative feedback about curation, Netflix&nbsp;
decided to fire their Chief Product Officer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:34.240
was responsible for the curation algorithm: Neil&nbsp;
Hunt. This was despite Neil being a personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:41.440
friend of Reed Hastings and an OG employee who had&nbsp;
been there since 1999. And that was a rather soft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.560
sacrifice. In mid 2021, Netflix fired 3 marketing&nbsp;
executives at the same time for not conforming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:53.080
the company’s policy of radical transparancy.&nbsp;
For perspective, that was half of their entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.920
executive marketing team. As you can see,&nbsp;
Netflix doesn’t hold back, but atleast Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.800
is completely transparent about that. They’re&nbsp;
cutthroat and their proud about it. The same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:16.983
however, cannot be said about their big tech&nbsp;
peers who often use much more underhanded tactics.

00:06:16.983 --> 00:06:19.720
GOOD COP BAD COP:
The king of underhanded tactics is none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:26.200
than Amazon who very much has a culture of churn&nbsp;
because that’s how boss Bezos likes it. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:31.920
Bezos isn’t a big fan of tenured employees because&nbsp;
he thinks that people simply get more comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.760
and lazier with time leading to a march to&nbsp;
mediocrity. As such, Amazon has a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.560
policies to constantly churn through employees&nbsp;
starting with PIPs or Performance Improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:49.200
Plans. Every year, Amazon has a policy of firing&nbsp;
the bottom 10% of their workforce. This way, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:55.400
quality of the workforce is always theoretically&nbsp;
improving. In reality, this often just leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:01.520
a bunch of toxicity, office politics, and bad&nbsp;
blood. In fact, it’s common practice for Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:07.240
managers to hire to fire which is exactly what&nbsp;
it sounds like. If you’re an Amazon manager with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.360
a solid team, it’s common to hire someone just&nbsp;
so that you can fire them at the end of the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.600
and preserve your core team. This also works&nbsp;
perfectly with Amazon’s compensation structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:22.520
which only pays out the majority of the stock&nbsp;
comp in the 3rd and 4th years. So, they’re able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:27.640
to get away with paying much less to employees&nbsp;
who get churned out. But, coming from Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:32.880
I don’t think any of that is really surprising.&nbsp;
What may be surprising, however, is the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.280
tactics that Google and Facebook employ. Unlike&nbsp;
Amazon or Netflix, Google and Facebook are much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:43.960
more sly in their approach as they like come of&nbsp;
as the good guy. As such, they tend to employ good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:49.320
cop strategies. For example, the main reason that&nbsp;
Google serves free breakfast, lunch, and dinner is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:55.360
not because they love feeding everyone great food&nbsp;
but to maximize on productivity. If great food is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.600
served on campus, it’s less likely that people&nbsp;
will go out for lunch. In fact, it’s likely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:06.240
a lot would simply work through lunch or atleast&nbsp;
spend a lot less time at lunch. Also, the timing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.200
of free food is positioned to increase the length&nbsp;
of the work day. For example, Google doesn’t start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:17.000
serving dinner till 6:30 to 8 pm. As such, if&nbsp;
you come into work at 8 am and wanna partake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:23.640
in free dinner, you can easily find yourself at&nbsp;
work from 8 am to 8 pm. It’s the same reason why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:29.360
Google offers or atleast used to offer perks like&nbsp;
free massages, haircuts, and ping pong tables. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.560
of these perks are designed to keep you at the&nbsp;
office for as long as possible, and hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:38.960
work for as long as possible. Even more employee&nbsp;
friendly policies like unlimited paid time off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:44.280
have much darker realities. When a company&nbsp;
offers unlimited paid time off, employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:50.720
feel a lot more guilty when taking days off. In&nbsp;
fact, the average employee with unlimited PTO only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:56.560
takes 11 days off per year, while the average&nbsp;
employee with limited PTO takes of 17 days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:02.800
and that’s just what you see at first glance. It&nbsp;
turns out that 42% of employees with unlimited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:08.440
PTO actually end up working even during their&nbsp;
vacation days, and it gets even worse. When you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.880
have limited PTO and you don’t use all of it, you&nbsp;
have the ability cash out this PTO for cash when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.880
you leave even if it’s because you got fired.&nbsp;
With unlimited PTO though, there is no accrued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.040
PTO meaning that there’s nothing to cash out&nbsp;
when you leave even though you barely took any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.600
vacation. After all, they hire the most talented&nbsp;
and competitive people in the world, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.720
dangle the opportunity to have even more power,&nbsp;
money, and influence in front of them. I think you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:45.302
can guess what sort of environment this would lead&nbsp;
which brings us into truth about big tech culture.

00:09:45.302 --> 00:09:47.720
EARNING YOUR WAGE:
As you can see, Netflix has the whole professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:53.520
athlete model, Amazon has the churn model, and&nbsp;
Google and Facebook have the good cop model. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:59.440
if this is the reality of working at big tech, why&nbsp;
do we never hear about this? Even more than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:04.480
why do we always hear about these companies&nbsp;
being the places in the world to work? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:10.520
this has a lot to do with selection bias and a&nbsp;
great analogy for this is AP testing. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:14.720
not familiar with AP tests, it’s what a lot&nbsp;
of American high school students take to earn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:21.240
college credit. The lowest score on the test is&nbsp;
a 1 and the highest score is a 5. One of the AP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:28.440
subjects with the lowest 5 rates is Physics 1 with&nbsp;
only 8% of students getting a 5. 27% of students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:34.640
actually get the lowest score, a 1. This isn’t&nbsp;
surprising given that Physics is an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:41.160
tough subject. You know what is surprising though?&nbsp;
One of the AP subjects with the highest 5 rates is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:49.080
Physics C E&amp;M, a much harder and advanced physics&nbsp;
class. 31% of test takers get a 5 on Physics C E&amp;M&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:56.440
and only 12% get a 1. A subject with even better 5&nbsp;
rates is Calculus BC where a whopping 42% of test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:03.560
takers get a 5 and only 6% get a 1. Does this mean&nbsp;
that Physics C and Calculus BC are way easier than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:09.160
Physics 1? Of course not. It’s just that only&nbsp;
super smart people take these advanced classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:14.320
in the first place and they end up doing really&nbsp;
well. It’s the same thing with these big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.840
companies. It’s not that these jobs are actually&nbsp;
super chill and pay super well. They’re actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:25.680
hypercompetitive and extremely keen on ensuring&nbsp;
that you earn your salary. But, only extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.400
talented hyper competitive individuals who thrive&nbsp;
in this sort of environment are actually hired to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:35.640
begin with. As such, big tech is actually much&nbsp;
more comparable to other high pressure fields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:40.840
like investment banking and hedge funds as opposed&nbsp;
to normal companies who happen to hire software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:47.200
engineers. And this is the truth that is often&nbsp;
lost when discussing careers at big tech. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:53.240
with any industry, the payout is proportional to&nbsp;
the expectations. With something like finance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:57.640
the high expectations are immediately&nbsp;
evident. You have to outperform the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:03.400
something that 90% of hedge fund managers fail&nbsp;
to do. With tech, the high expectations aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:09.200
as evident from the outside, but believe&nbsp;
me, there is no free lunch at big tech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:15.680
both literally and figuratively. And that is&nbsp;
the true price of working at big tech. Atleast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:20.200
these companies pay massive salaries to&nbsp;
compensate for that. Oh wait, nevermind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:24.240
they’re making moves to dramatically decrease&nbsp;
pay. Check out this video to learn more.

